o the rest.\n\n2.All Grand Slam champions are professional tennis players.So if someone is a Grand Slam champion, then they are a professional tennis player.\n\n3.All Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities.So Oscar-nominated actors are a subset of celebrities.\n\n4.All professional tennis players are athletes.So if you\'re a pro tennis player, you\'re an athlete.\n\n5.If a person is a celebrity, then they are well paid.So celebrities → well paid.\n\n6.If a person is an athlete, then they are famous.Athletes → famous.\n\n7.All well-paid people live in tax havens.So well paid → tax haven.\n\n8.The last statement says, "If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in well paid."Wait, that last part seems a bit off.The original says: "If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in well paid."Wait, "lives in well paid"?That might be a typo.Because the previous statements mention "live in tax havens" and "well paid people live in tax havens."So maybe it\'s supposed to say "lives in a tax haven"?Because otherwise "well paid" is an adjective, not a place.So perhaps there\'s a translation error here.Let me check again.\n\nThe original problem says: "If a person is a celebrity then they are well paid.If a person is an athlete then they are famous.All well-paid people live in tax havens.If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in well paid.".\n\nHmm, the last sentence\'s conclusion is "lives in well paid", which is probably a mistake.Since the prior premise was that well-paid people live in tax havens, maybe the intended conclusion is that Djokovic lives in a tax haven.Alternatively, maybe it\'s a misstatement.Let me assume that the last part was intended to say "lives in a tax haven", because otherwise it doesn\'t make sense.Alternatively, maybe the problem is as written.But I need to work with what\'s given.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps "lives in well paid" is a typo, and it should be "lives in a tax haven".Let me see.Let me 