      "text": " B) False? Wait, no. Wait, let me recheck. Wait, the problem says \"Karen does not download 'Black Mirror'\". So Karen did not download \"Black Mirror\". Since the iff is between binge-watching and downloading, then not downloading implies not binge-watching. So she didn't binge-watch it. Therefore, she won't share it. So the question is asking if \"Karen will share 'Black Mirror' to Lisa\" is true. Since the conditions required (her binge-watching) are not met, then the statement is false. Therefore, the answer is B) False. So the correct option is B. I think that's right. I don't see any contradictions here.\n</think>\n\nB) False\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Premises:**\n   - \"Black Mirror\" is a Netflix show (given).\n   - Karen does not download \"Black Mirror\" (given).\n   - The statement \"If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it\" means:\n     - **Binge-watch \u2192 Download (sufficient condition)**\n     - **Download \u2192 Binge-watch (necessary condition)**\n   - From Karen not downloading \"Black Mirror\", by the biconditional, she also did **not binge-watch** it.\n   - The rule \"If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa\" requires binge-watching as a prerequisite for sharing.\n\n2. **Analysis:**\n   - Since Karen did not binge-watch \"Black Mirror\" (as inferred from not downloading), the antecedent (\"if\" part) of the sharing rule fails.\n   - A failed antecedent means the consequent (sharing) **does not occur**.\n   - Thus, Karen will **not** share \"Black Mirror\" to Lisa.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   - The statement \"Karen will share 'Black Mirror' to Lisa\" is **false**.\n\n**Answer:** B) False",
