)).\n\n2.∀x (A(x) → P(x)).\n\n3.∀x (Plane(x) → E(x)).\n\n4.∀x (B707(x) → Plane(x)).\n\n5.If ~A(Greyhound) → ¬∃y (Plane(y) ∧ G(y)).\n\nWait, but in premise 5, is "Greyhound" a constant (a specific entity) or a predicate?Probably a constant here.So "Greyhound" is a specific entity, like a company.Then the fifth premise is: If Greyhound is not an airline (~A(Greyhound)), then there are no planes that are Greyhound (i.e., no plane y such that G(y))..\n\nWait, perhaps the "Greyhound planes" are planes that are owned by Greyhound.So, if Greyhound is not an airline, then it can\'t own planes?That might be the intended meaning.\n\nNow, the question is whether "A Greyhound is a Boeing 707."Assuming "Greyhound" here is the same entity as in premise 5, then the statement would be "Greyhound is a Boeing 707," i.e., B707(Greyhound)..\n\nAlternatively, if "Greyhound" is a predicate, then the statement could be ∃x (G(x) ∧ B707(x)), meaning there exists something that is a Greyhound and a Boeing 707.\n\nLet me consider both interpretations.\n\nFirst interpretation: The statement is "Greyhound is a Boeing 707" (i.e., B707(Greyhound)).\n\nIf that\'s the case, then:.\n\n- If Greyhound is a Boeing 707, then it is a plane (from premise 4: B707 → Plane).\n\n- Therefore, as a plane, it must be empty (premise 3: Plane → E).So E(Greyhound) is true.\n\n- But from premise 2 and 5: If Greyhound is an airline (A(Greyhound)), then it can transport passengers (P(Greyhound)), which would require ¬E(Greyhound) (from premise 1).But if it\'s a plane, then E(Greyhound) is true, so P(Greyhound) must be false..\n\nBut if Greyhound is both an airline and a plane (which would be possible only if the entity is both), then we have a contradiction: A(Greyhound) requires P(Greyhound) → ¬E(Greyhound), but being a plane requires E(Greyhound).Therefore, such an entity cannot exist..\n\nAlternatively, if Greyhound is not an airline, then from premise 5, there are no planes that are Greyhound.But if Greyhound is a Boeing 707 (a plane), then that would violate premise 5\'s consequent if Greyhound isn\'t an airline..\n\nTherefore, if we suppose that "Greyhound is a Boeing 707" (i.e., B707(Greyhound)), then:.\n\n- Since it\'s a Boeing 707, it\'s a plane → E(Greyhound).\n\n- If Greyhound is not an airline (~A(Greyhound)), then by premise 5, there are no planes that are Greyhound.But Greyhound itself is a plane (as a Boeing 707), so this would mean that such an entity can\'t exist because it would violate the consequent of premise 5. .\n\n- Alternatively, if Greyhound is an airline (A(Greyhound)), then as an airline, it can transport passengers (P(Greyhound)), so by premise 1, it\'s not empty (¬E(Greyhound)).But since it\'s a plane (Boeing 707), it must be empty (E(Greyhound)).Contradiction again..\n\nTherefore, the statement "Greyhound is a Boeing 707" leads to a contradiction regardless of whether Greyhound is an airline or not.Therefore, it must be false..\n\nAlternatively, if the statement is "There exists a Greyhound that is a Boeing 707" (∃x (G(x) ∧ B707(x))), then:.\n\nLet’s suppose such an x exists.Then:.\n\n- x is a Boeing 707 → x is a plane → E(x).\n\n- The existence of x (a plane that is a Greyhound) requires that Greyhound is an airline (from premise 5\'s contrapositive: if there exists a Greyhound plane, then A(Greyhound) must be true).\n\nIf something can transport passengers, then it is not empty, which is okay because the plane is separate from the airline. The airline itself is not a plane, so its emptiness doesn\'t conflict. If Greyhound is an airline, then as an airline, it can transport passengers. The first premise says that if something can transport passengers, it is not empty. All planes are empty, so they can\'t transport passengers. Premise 3 says all planes are empty. Premise 1 says if something can transport passengers, it is not empty. Premise 2 says all airlines can transport passengers. This is a contradiction.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]  