erson is either a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor.So for Djokovic, he must be one or the other.Since he\'s a real person, in reality, he is a Grand Slam champion (a tennis player), but we have to go purely by the given information, not real-world knowledge.\n\nAssuming the context is fictional unless stated otherwise.So we have to work with the given statements only.\n\nSo first, Djokovic must be either a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor.Let\'s see what other premises can help.\n\nIf he is a Grand Slam champion, then from premise 2, he is a professional tennis player.Then from premise 4, he is an athlete.From premise 6, being an athlete makes him famous.\n\nAlternatively, if he were an Oscar-nominated actor, then from premise 3, he would be a celebrity.Then from premise 5, he would be well paid, and from premise 7, well-paid people live in tax havens.But since the question is about him not living in a tax haven, that would conflict.\n\nBut let\'s see step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s consider the two possibilities for Djokovic:\n\nCase 1: Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion.\n\nThen:\n\n- He is a professional tennis player (from 2).\n\n- Therefore, he is an athlete (from 4).\n\n- Being an athlete, by premise 6, he is famous.\n\n- Now, from the last premise: If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in well paid.Wait, that\'s the exact wording.But "lives in well paid" is not a place.Maybe that\'s a mistranslation.Let me check again.The user wrote: "If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in well paid."Maybe "well paid" here refers to being well-paid, so the conclusion is that he is well paid.Because the previous premises link being well-paid to living in tax havens.So perhaps the last premise is a conditional that if he is famous and an athlete, then he is well paid.That would make sense.Maybe it\'s a typo where "lives in well paid" should be "is well paid".\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it\'s trying to say that he lives in a tax haven because he\'s well paid.But given the exact wording, perhaps the last premise is an additional conditional that if he is famous and an athlete, then he lives in a tax haven (since well-paid people do).Let me try to parse it again.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the last statement is a separate conditional that if he is famous and an athlete, then he lives in a tax haven.Because the previous premise (7) says all well-paid people live in tax havens.So if he is an athlete, being an athlete makes him famous (premise 6).But how does that lead to being well-paid?\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nIn case 1: Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion.\n\n- So he is a professional tennis player (2).\n\n- Therefore athlete (4).\n\n- Therefore famous (6).\n\nNow, to get to well-paid, he must be a celebrity (since celebrities are well-paid).But he is a Grand Slam champion, so unless he is also an Oscar-nominated actor (which he can\'t be, because of the first premise which says either/or), he can\'t be a celebrity.Wait, hold on.Wait, the first premise says "A person is either a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor."So each person must be one or the other, but not both.So if he is a Grand Slam champion, he cannot be an Oscar-nominated actor.Therefore, he is not a celebrity via premise 3 (since Oscar nominees are celebrities, but he\'s not an Oscar nominee).So unless there\'s another way for him to be a celebrity, he can\'t be one.Wait, but the premises don\'t state that only Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities.Maybe there are other celebrities, but according to premise 3, all Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities, but that doesn\'t mean that only they are.Hmm, but maybe the only way to be a celebrity here is through being an Oscar nominee?Not necessarily.The premise says "All Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities," but that doesn\'t exclude others from being celebrities.However, since he is a Grand Slam champion, unless there\'s another way, he mi