      "text": " B) False.\n\nWait, let me check again.\n\nSuppose Rock is a cute rabbit: then Rock is an animal (rabbit), so not a turtle (since turtles are non-animals?), but if turtles are non-animals, then Rock can't be a turtle. So the conclusion \"Rock is a turtle and skittish\" would be false. Therefore, in this scenario, the implication would be false, which would make the overall statement invalid (i.e., not always true). So the statement is false because there exists a case where the antecedent is true but the consequent is false. Therefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another angle.\n\nWait, let's consider if Rock is a turtle. Since some turtles exist, Rock could be a turtle. If Rock is a turtle, then Rock is not an animal (since animals are only rabbits or squirrels). Since Rock is a turtle, it's possible that Rock is cute (since the context doesn't say turtles can't be cute). Also, Rock is still, so if Rock is skittish, then by the fourth premise, it can't be still. But Rock is still, so if Rock is skittish, that would be a contradiction. Therefore, if Rock is a turtle and skittish, then it can't be still. But Rock is still, so Rock cannot be skittish. Therefore, if Rock is a turtle, it can't be skittish (since it's still). Therefore, the conclusion \"Rock is a turtle and skittish\" would be impossible if Rock is a turtle. Wait, so if Rock is a turtle, then to satisfy the conclusion, Rock must be skittish and a turtle, but being skittish would require not being still, but Rock is still. So if Rock is a turtle and still, it can't be skittish. Therefore, if the antecedent is Rock is cute and still (so Rock is a cute turtle, since it's a turtle and still?), then the conclusion would require Rock to be skittish, which it can't be. Therefore, even if Rock is a turtle, the conclusion can't hold.\n\nWait, let me retrace.\n\nSuppose Rock is a turtle (so non-animal), and suppose Rock is cute (so the antecedent \"Rock is cute and still\" is true). Then the conclusion is \"Rock is a turtle and skittish.\" Since Rock is a turtle, that part is true, but is Rock skittish?\n\nIf Rock is skittish, then by premise 4, Rock cannot be still. But Rock is still, so Rock cannot be skittish. Therefore, in this scenario, the conclusion would be \"turtle and skittish\", but skittish is false. Therefore, the conclusion is false. Therefore, even if Rock is a cute turtle and still, the conclusion is false. Therefore, in this case, the implication's antecedent is true, and consequent is false, making the implication false.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Rock could be a squirrel?\n\nWait, if Rock is an animal, it has to be a rabbit or a squirrel. Suppose Rock is a squirrel. Since all squirrels are skittish (premise 5), so Rock would be skittish. But skittish implies not still (premise4), so Rock can't be still. But Rock is given as still. Therefore, Rock cannot be a squirrel. So if Rock is an animal, it can't be a squirrel (since that would contradict stillness), so Rock must be a rabbit.\n\nSo, if Rock is an animal, then it must be a rabbit. Then, since rabbits are cute, Rock is cute. So in that case, the antecedent (Rock is cute and still) is true (since Rock is a rabbit, so cute, and Rock is still). But the conclusion requires Rock to be a turtle and skittish. But Rock is a rabbit (so not a turtle), so conclusion is false. Therefore, this scenario also makes the implication false.\n\nAlternatively, if Rock is not an animal (so a turtle), then the antecedent requires Rock to be cute and still. Suppose Rock is a cute turtle and still. Then the conclusion is Rock is a turtle and skittish. But skittish would require not still, but Rock is still. Therefore, the conclusion is false, so implication is false again.\n\nAlternatively, what if Rock is neither an animal nor a turtle? The context says some turtles exist, but doesn't state that all non-animals are turtles. So maybe Rock could be a different non-animal. But even