      "text": "2.\n\nWait, but I'm confused because I initially had a different conclusion. Let me retrace.\n\nWait, the contrapositive of \"All P are L\" is \"If not L, then not P\". So actually, if someone does not work in the library (\u00acL), then they cannot have a part-time job. Wait no, the contrapositive is: If you don't work in the library (\u00acL), then you are not in P. So \u00acL \u2192 \u00acP. Because the original is P\u2192 L. So contrapositive is \u00acL \u2192 \u00acP.\n\nAh, that's where I messed up earlier. So if James is not in L, then he cannot have P. Because \u00acL implies \u00acP.\n\nTherefore, since James is not in L (because he is in CS), then he cannot have P. Therefore, the statement \"James does not have a part-time job\" is true. So the correct answer is A) True.\n\nWait, so where did I go wrong in the second analysis?\n\nBecause I thought that \"if you have P, you must be in L\", which is correct. So if you are not in L, then you can't have P. Therefore, since James is not in L (because in CS), then he can't have P. So the answer is A) True.\n\nBut earlier I was confused because I thought contrapositive might have been flipped. So the correct conclusion is A.\n\nWait, let me check the steps again step by step.\n\nJames is in the lab \u2192 instructed by David \u2192 taking DB \u2192 in CS (from premises 5,4,3).\n\nTherefore, James is in CS. Therefore, from premise 1, since he is in CS, he cannot work in the library (because if you work in library, you can't be in CS; contrapositive: if you are in CS, you can't work in library).\n\nNow, the question is whether James has a part-time job (P). The premise says: All P are L (P\u2192L). So if James had P, he would have to be in L. But he can't be in L. Therefore, he cannot have P. Therefore, it is true that he does not have P. So the answer is A) True.\n\nYes. The confusion earlier was when I thought the contrapositive might be \u00acL\u2192P, but that's wrong. The contrapositive of P\u2192L is \u00acL\u2192\u00acP. So that's right. Thus, since James is not in L (\u00acL), he must not have P (\u00acP). Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nSo the correct answer is A.\n</think>\n\nA) True\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **James works in the lab (Premise 6)** \u2192 By Premise 5, he is instructed by Professor David (Lab \u2192 Dv).\n2. **Instructed by David (Dv)** \u2192 By Premise 4, James takes the database course (Dv \u2192 D).\n3. **Taking the database course (D)** \u2192 By Premise 3, he is in the computer science department (D \u2192 C).\n4. **In CS (C)** \u2192 By Premise 1 (contrapositive), he cannot work in the library (C \u2192 \u00acL).\n5. **Part-time job (P) requires working in the library (Premise 2: P \u2192 L)** \u2192 Since James cannot work in the library (\u00acL), he cannot have a part-time job (\u00acL \u2192 \u00acP).\n\n**Conclusion:** James does not have a part-time job. The statement is **True (Option A)**.",
