      "text": " Jack neither works in the lab nor has a part-time job. Since all lab workers are David's students and thus take the database course, Jack isn't in that group. Also, since he doesn't have a part-time job, he isn't a library worker (though he might be one without the job, but that's not stated). But to take the database course, he needs to be in CS. There's no information linking Jack to the CS department or being a student of David. Therefore, it's uncertain whether he takes the course. So the answer should be C) Uncertain.\n\nWait, wait, but let me think again. Let me see if there's any possible way he could be in the database course.\n\nSuppose Jack is in CS department but not in David's class. Then he could still be taking the course if there are other ways to take it, but the premises only say that all database students are in CS, but don't say all CS students must take it. So it's possible but not necessary.\n\nAlternatively, he might not be in CS, so can't take it.\n\nSo since we don't know his department, we can't confirm. Therefore, the answer is uncertain. So C.\n\nWait but maybe another angle: The only students taking the database course are those instructed by David? No, because point 4 says all David's students take it, but there could be others in CS who take it without being David's students. So unless there's another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe all database course students are taught by David? No, the premises don't say that.\n\nSo, there are two possibilities: Jack could be in CS and taking the course, or not.\n\nSince we have no info on his department or professor, it's uncertain. So the answer is C.\n\nWait, but Jack could also be in another department, so then he can't take it. But since we don't know his department, the answer is uncertain. So the correct answer is C.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. The question is whether the statement \"Jack takes the database course\" is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nIf there's a possibility that he could take it (if he's in CS and in the course) and also a possibility that he doesn't, then it's uncertain. So yes, C is correct.\n\nWait, but another thought: Since Jack does not work in the library, does that mean he could be in CS? Because the first premise says library workers are not in CS. So if Jack doesn't work in the library, then the premise doesn't restrict his department. So he could be in CS or not.\n\nTherefore, his department is unknown. Thus, the course is uncertain. So the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nC) Uncertain\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Premises Recap:**\n   - Library workers \u2260 Computer Science (CS) students.\n   - Part-time job holders \u2192 work in the library.\n   - Database course students \u2192 CS department.\n   - Professor David\u2019s students \u2192 take the database course.\n   - Lab workers \u2192 instructed by David \u2192 take the database course \u2192 CS department.\n   - James works in the lab (thus takes the course and is in CS).\n   - **Jack:** No part-time job, no lab work.\n\n2. **Analysis of Jack:**\n   - **Department:** \n     - Jack doesn\u2019t work in the library (since he lacks a part-time job, and part-time job holders are library workers). However, the first premise only states library workers are excluded from CS, not that non-library workers *must* be in CS. Thus, Jack could be in CS or another department.\n   - **Taking the Database Course:** \n     - To take the course, Jack must be in CS (Premise 3). \n     - Even if in CS, Jack might not take it unless instructed by David (Premise 4/5) or another route (no premises require all CS students to take it). \n     - Jack isn\u2019t in the lab (so not David\u2019s student via Premise 5), but David might have other students outside the lab. However, there\u2019s no info linking Jack to David or any other requirement for the course.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   - Jack\u2019s department and course enrollment are unknown due to insufficient information. While possible (if in CS and taking the course) or impossible (if not in CS), neither can be confirmed. \n\n**Answer:** C) Uncertain",
