e B is false.\n\n- So that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, if S is false:\n\n- No constraints except from other premises.\n\nThus, there is no contradiction either way.\n\nTherefore, Luke could be in case 2 with S true or S false, so we cannot determine the truth value of S. Hence, the answer is uncertain (C).\n\nI think that's correct. Initially I was confused because I thought maybe there was a hidden constraint, but after careful analysis, the conclusion is that it's uncertain.\n</think>\n\nTo determine if Luke spends a lot of time with his siblings (S), analyze the given premises and Luke's conditions:\n\n1. **Premises**:\n   - Twins/triplets (T) \u2192 spends time with siblings (S).\n   - Same-age siblings (SA) \u2192 twins/triplets (T).\n   - Complains (C) \u2192 same-age siblings (SA).\n   - Not living at home (\u00acL) \u2192 no strong bonds (\u00acB).\n   - Spends time with siblings (S) \u2192 does not live at home (\u00acL).\n   - Luke is either (T \u2227 B) or (\u00acT \u2227 \u00acB).\n\n2. **Case Analysis**:\n   - **Case 1 (T \u2227 B)**:\n     - T implies S (from premise 1), so Luke spends time with siblings (S = True).\n     - B implies living at home (L = True) (from premise 4).\n     - Conflict: S = True requires \u00acL (premise 5), but L = True contradicts \u00acL. Thus, **Case 1 is impossible**.\n   - **Case 2 (\u00acT \u2227 \u00acB)**:\n     - T = False \u2192 SA = False (from premise 2\u2019s contrapositive).\n     - B = False \u2192 no constraints on L (premise 4 allows L = True or False).\n     - S can be **True or False** without contradiction:\n       - If S = True, then \u00acL must hold (L = False), no conflict with B = False.\n       - If S = False, premises are satisfied regardless of L. \n\nSince Luke must be in **Case 2**, and S is not determined by the premises, the statement\u2019s truth value cannot be resolved. \n\n**Answer: C) Uncertain**",
