Mars" is true, false, or uncertain.Let me try to structure this step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s consider the possibilities for Marvin\'s origin.He can be from Earth, from Mars, or from some other place.But given the premises, maybe we can narrow it down.\n\nStarting with the first part of the question\'s statement: "Marvin is neither a human.." So if we can determine whether he is a human, that\'s part of it.And "nor from Mars" — so also need to see if he is from Mars or not.\n\nLet me consider possible scenarios.\n\nCase 1: Suppose Marvin is from Earth.Then, according to "Everyone from Earth is a human," he would be human.But the statement claims he\'s neither human nor from Mars.So if he\'s from Earth, then he is human, which would make the statement false.But we have to see if this case is possible.\n\nCase 2: Suppose Marvin is from Mars.Then, by the second premise, he is an alien, and since all aliens are extraterrestrial, he\'s an extraterrestrial.Since no extraterrestrial is human, he can\'t be human.But the statement says he is neither human nor from Mars.Wait, if he is from Mars, then the second part of the statement ("nor from Mars") would be false.So the entire statement would be false in this case.\n\nCase 3: Suppose Marvin is from neither Earth nor Mars.Then he\'s from somewhere else.Let\'s see.If he\'s not from Earth, then by the last premise, "If Marvin is not from Earth, then Marvin is an extraterrestrial."So he would be an extraterrestrial.Since he\'s an extraterrestrial, he can\'t be human (from the third premise).So in this case, he would be neither human nor from Mars (since he\'s from another planet).That would make the statement true.\n\nBut we need to check if there\'s any constraints on where else he can be from.The premises don\'t restrict other planets except Mars.So he could be from Venus, for example.But the key is, if he\'s not from Earth, he must be an extraterrestrial.So in that case, he\'s an extraterrestrial, so not human, and not from Mars (since we\'re in case 3 where he\'s not from Mars either).So that scenario would make the statement true.\n\nBut we need to see if all possibilities must lead to the statement being true, or if there\'s a case where it\'s false or uncertain.\n\nWait, but the problem is asking if the statement is true, false, or uncertain given the premises.So we have to see if the premises necessarily make the statement true, necessarily false, or if it could be either.\n\nLet me try to structure this with logic.\n\nLet me denote the propositions:.\n\nLet E = Marvin is from Earth.\n\nM = Marvin is from Mars.\n\nH = Marvin is human.\n\nA = Marvin is an alien (since "aliens are extraterrestrial" but maybe the term is interchangeable here?).\n\nWait, the first premise says all aliens are extraterrestrial.So aliens are a subset of extraterrestrials.But the second premise says if from Mars, then alien.So Martians are aliens, hence extraterrestrials.But extraterrestrial could include more than just aliens?Wait, maybe the terms are being used in a specific way here.Let me check again.\n\nWait, the first sentence says "All aliens are extraterrestrial."So "aliens" here might be a specific category, and extraterrestrial is a broader category?Or maybe "extraterrestrial" is the same as "alien"?The wording is a bit ambiguous, but in the problem\'s context, perhaps "aliens" are extraterrestrials, so they are the same.Wait, but the problem says "If someone is from Mars, then they are aliens."So Martians are a subset of aliens, which are extraterrestrials.But the third premise says "No extraterrestrial is human."So aliens (which are extraterrestrials) can\'t be humans.So if someone is an alien (like from Mars), they can\'t be human..\n\nAlso, everyone from Earth is human.So Earthlings are humans, and non-Earthlings may be extraterrestrials.\n\nNow, the key is to determine Marvin\'s status.\n\nThe problem says "Marvin cannot be from Earth and from Mars."So ¬(E ∧ M).So he can\'t be both.\n\nNow