Remote.\n   - Managers cannot work remote (Manager \u2192 \u00acWorkRemote).\n   - **James' Condition:** Either (Manager \u2227 Appear) **OR** (\u00acManager \u2227 \u00acAppear).\n\n2. **Analyzing Cases for James:**\n   - **Case 1 (Manager \u2227 Appear):**\n     - If James lunches at company (LunchCo), he must have scheduled a meeting (from LunchCo \u2192 Schedule). Since he appears, this is possible.\n     - If LunchHome, then WorkRemote. But managers can\u2019t work remote (\u00acWorkRemote). Contradiction.\n     - Thus, **LunchCo is required**, making \"James does not lunch at company\" **False** here.\n\n   - **Case 2 (\u00acManager \u2227 \u00acAppear):**\n     - If LunchCo, then Schedule (from LunchCo \u2192 Schedule). But \u00acAppear contradicts Schedule \u2192 Appear. Thus, LunchCo is impossible.\n     - Therefore, **LunchHome is required**, making \"James does not lunch at company\" **True** here.\n\n3. **Uncertainty Arises:**\n   - The problem states James is in **either Case 1 OR Case 2**, but does not specify which. Hence, the truth of the statement depends on his actual case.\n   - Since we cannot determine whether James is a manager who appears or a non-manager who doesn\u2019t, the statement\u2019s truth is **uncertain**.\n\n**Conclusion:** The answer is **C) Uncertain**.",
