ce this to be impossible.\n\nCase 2: Suppose John does NOT have at least one child (¬Child_J).Then, what do we know?.\n\nWe need to see if this is possible.Let\'s see if there are any constraints.\n\nFirst, if John does not have a child, does that affect anything?Let\'s see.\n\nPremise 1 is about people with weddings needing to have children, but if John doesn\'t have a wedding, then premise 1 doesn\'t apply to him.So, John could be without a child and without a wedding.Alternatively, he might have a wedding, but that would require him to have a child (premise 1).But if he doesn\'t have a child, then he can\'t have a wedding (because of premise 1\'s contrapositive: ¬Child → ¬Wedding).Wait, yes.Let\'s think:.\n\nPremise 1: Wedding → Child.Contrapositive: ¬Child → ¬Wedding.So, if John has no child, he cannot have a wedding.Therefore, in case 2 (¬Child_J), John cannot have a wedding.\n\nTherefore, in both cases (whether he has a child or not), he cannot have a wedding unless he has a child, but if he has a child, he can\'t have a wedding either (from premise 5).Wait, no.Wait, let me clarify:.\n\nWait, if he has a child (case 1), then premise 5 says he can\'t have a wedding.If he doesn\'t have a child (case 2), then premise 1\'s contrapositive says he can\'t have a wedding.Therefore, in either case, John cannot have a wedding.So, John does not have a wedding..\n\nIs that correct?Let me confirm:.\n\n- If Child_J → ¬Wedding_J (premise 5).\n- If ¬Child_J → ¬Wedding_J (from premise 1\'s contrapositive).\n\nTherefore, regardless of whether John has a child or not, he cannot have a wedding.Therefore, John does not have a wedding..\n\nTherefore, John cannot have a wedding.Therefore, the antecedent of premise 6 is about "John has a wedding that is well-attended".Since John cannot have a wedding at all, that antecedent is false, so premise 6 is vacuously true.So, premise 6 doesn\'t give us any new information about John\'s child status.\n\nNow, let\'s see if there are any other constraints from the premises that might affect whether John has a child.\n\nWe also have premise 3 and 4, which are about weddings in July and their Paris honeymoons.Since John cannot have a wedding, those premises don\'t directly affect him, unless there\'s some indirect connection.\n\nWait, but let\'s see if there are any implications from the other premises.For example, if John traveled to Paris for a honeymoon, then he must have a wedding (premise 2).But since John cannot have a wedding, he cannot have traveled to Paris for a honeymoon either.So, Paris_J → Wedding_J → ¬Child_J (from premise 5\'s contrapositive), but since Wedding_J is impossible, Paris_J is impossible.Therefore, John cannot have a Paris honeymoon either.\n\nBut the question is about whether he has a child.Let\'s see if there\'s any way to determine this.\n\nWe know from the premises that John cannot have a wedding, so that doesn\'t help.Let\'s consider if there\'s any other path.\n\nSuppose John does have a child (case 1).Then, he can\'t have a wedding or a Paris honeymoon.That\'s okay.Alternatively, if he doesn\'t have a child (case 2), then he also can\'t have a wedding..\n\nThe problem is asking whether "John has at least one child" is true, false, or uncertain.To determine this, we need to see if either case leads to a contradiction or if both are possible.\n\nAre there any premises that could lead us to conclude one way or the other?.\n\nLet me check premise 4 again: "Some weddings in July are well-attended."This is an existential statement, but it\'s about some weddings in general, not necessarily John\'s.Since John cannot have a wedding, this doesn\'t affect him directly.However, maybe there\'s a connection via premise 6?.\n\nWait, premise 6 says: If John has a wedding that is well-attended, then..But since John cannot have a wedding at all, that\'s irrelevant.So, the only premises affecting John\'s child status are premises 1,5, and the contrapositives.\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s a different angle.Let\'s suppose that John does not have a child (case 2).Then, he can\'t have a wedding.Is there any other constraint that would require him to have a child?.\n\nAlternatively, suppose that John does have a child (case 1).Is that possible without contradiction?.\n\nYes.Because in case 1, he has a child, so he can\'t have a wedding (by premise 5), which is okay because he doesn\'t have one.So, that scenario is possible.\n\nGiven the premises, it is certain that John must have a child in order to have a wedding and Paris honeymoon, as well as attend a well-attended July wedding. Thus, the answer is True (A).. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
