as neither a well-attended wedding (since no wedding at all) nor travels to Paris. Hence the statement is true (A).\n\nWait, but what about the sixth premise? It says if John has a well-attended wedding, then... but he can't have any wedding, so that implication holds vacuously. So no problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) True. But I have to make sure there's no loophole.\n\nWait, is there a way for John to have a wedding in July without having a child? But no, because any wedding requires a child (premise 1). So even a July wedding would need C, which would then forbid W via premise 5. So that's impossible.\n\nSo all possibilities lead to the conclusion that John has neither. Therefore the correct answer is A.\n\nBut why did I get confused earlier? Because I misread premise 5 initially, which led me to an opposite conclusion. Now with the correct reading, it's clear.\n\nSo the final answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but let me check one more time.\n\nPremise 5: If John has a child, then he doesn't have a wedding or travel to Paris.\n\nBut if he doesn't have a child, does he have a wedding? No, because premise 1 requires a child for a wedding. So regardless of whether he has a child or not, he can't have a wedding, so no well-attended one. And since he can't have a wedding, he can't travel to Paris (due to premise 2). So both parts are false, meaning the statement \"John has neither...\" is true.\n\nYes, so the correct answer is A. But the original question lists the options as A, B, C. The user's options are A) True, B) False, C) Uncertain.\n\nThus, the correct answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe problem involves determining the truth of the statement: \"John has neither a wedding that is well-attended nor does he travel to Paris for his honeymoon.\" Here's the analysis:\n\n### Key Premises:\n1. **W \u2192 C**: If John has a wedding (W), he must have at least one child (C).\n2. **PH \u2192 W**: If John travels to Paris for his honeymoon (PH), he must have a wed