e a punctuation issue, but I think the parsing is correct.\n\nAnother angle: Suppose someone misapplies the logic and thinks that since Tom is an Animal Lover, the consequent's \u00acA is false, so the entire consequent is false, but since the antecedent is also false, does that affect anything? No, because in implication, only when the antecedent is true and consequent is false does the implication fail. Here, antecedent is false, so implication holds. So the answer is indeed A) True.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires evaluating the truth value of the statement: \"If Tom is not a grumpy person and mean to animals, then Tom is neither a grumpy person nor an Animal Lover.\" based on the given premises. Here's the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n### **Premises Analysis:**\n1. **\"No one nice to animals is mean to animals.\"**  \n   - Translates to: If someone is nice to animals (\\(N\\)), they are not mean to animals (\\(\\neg M\\)).  \n   - \\(N \\rightarrow \\neg M\\).\n\n2. **\"Some grumpy people are mean to animals.\"**  \n   - There exists overlap between grumpy people (\\(G\\)) and those mean to animals (\\(M\\)), but this does not apply to Tom directly.\n\n3. **\"All animal lovers are nice to animals.\"**  \n   - Animal lovers (\\(A\\)) are a subset of those nice to animals (\\(N\\)):  \n     \\(A \\rightarrow N\\).\n\n4. **\"If someone owns a pet, then they love animals.\"**  \n   - Pet owners (\\(P\\)) are animal lovers (\\(A\\)):  \n     \\(P \\rightarrow A\\).\n\n5. **\"Tom is a pet owner.\"**  \n   - Tom is a pet owner (\\(P\\)), so by premise 4, he is an animal lover (\\(A\\)):  \n     \\(A = \\text{True}\\).\n\n### **Tom's Attributes:**\n- From premise 5 and 4: Tom is an **animal lover (\\(A\\))**.  \n- From premise 3: Since Tom is an animal lover, he is **nice to animals (\\(N\\))**.  \n- From premise 1: Since Tom is nice to animals (\\(N\\)), he **cannot be mean to animals** (\\(\\neg M\\)).  \n\n### **Evaluating the Statement:**\nThe statement to evaluate is:  \n**\"If Tom is not a grumpy person and mean to animals \\((\\neg G \\land M)\\), then Tom is neither a grumpy person nor an Animal Lover \\((\\neg G \\land \\neg A)\\).\"**\n\n",
