 within the set of mammals.\n   - **Some pets are not mammals**: This indicates there exists at least one pet outside the mammal category.\n\n2. **Key Considerations**:\n   - The non-mammal pets cannot be cats (since all cats are mammals).\n   - The mammals category includes pets that could potentially be cats. The premises do not explicitly state whether any pets are cats or not.\n   - The statement \"No pets are cats\" would require that **no overlap exists** between pets and cats. However:\n     - The premises permit scenarios where pets could include cats (as cats are mammals, which is allowed), making the statement **false**.\n     - Alternatively, if no pets are cats, the statement would be **true**.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Since the premises neither confirm nor deny the existence of cats among pets, the truth of \"No pets are cats\" cannot be definitively determined. It depends on unaddressed information (e.g., whether any pets are cats).\n\n**Answer**: C) Uncertain",
