t this is a standard syllogism question, it's more likely that the answer is True. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nA) True\n\nThe context states two universal affirmatives: \"All humans are mortal\" and \"All Greeks are humans.\" In traditional syllogistic logic, these premises imply that the category \"Greeks\" is non-empty (existential import). Since all Greeks are humans and all humans are mortal, it follows that all Greeks are mortal. Therefore, \"Some Greeks are mortal\" must be true because there exists at least one Greek (due to existential import), and that Greek is mortal. \n\nIn modern logic, without assuming existential import, the conclusion\u2019s truth depends on whether Greeks exist. However, since the question is framed as a standard logical reasoning problem\u2014common in syllogism exercises\u2014the traditional interpretation applies. Thus, the correct answer is **A) True**.",
