tain.\n\nWait but let me check again. Suppose James is a manager and appears, then he can't have lunch at home, so he must have lunch at the company. So the statement is true.\n\nIf he is not a manager and not appearing, then he can't have lunch at the company (since that would require scheduling a meeting and thus appearing), so he must have lunch at home, so the statement is false.\n\nSince both options are possible, the statement's truth value is uncertain. So the answer is C.\n</think>\n\nBased on the given information, we analyze the two scenarios for James:\n\n**Case 1:** James is a **manager** and **appears in the company today**.  \n- As a manager, he cannot work remotely.  \n- If he had lunch at home, he would have to work remotely (contradicting his role as a manager).  \n- Therefore, he must have lunch **in the company**.  \n\n**Case 2:** James is **not a manager** and **does not appear in the company today**.  \n- Since he does not appear, he did not schedule a meeting (as scheduling a meeting would require him to appear).  \n- If he had lunch in the company, he would have had to schedule a meeting (which he didn\u2019t), so he **cannot have lunch in the company**.  \n- Thus, he must have lunch **at home**.  \n\nSince the problem states James must be in **either Case 1 or Case 2**, but we cannot determine which one applies, the truth of the statement \"James has lunch in the company\" depends on his unknown scenario.  \n\n**Answer:** C) Uncertain",
