ires (they suck blood), but space being a vacuum (the physical property) would also \"suck\" in the same way vacuums do (appliances). But then, if space \"sucks,\" does that make it a vampire? Not necessarily.\n\nThe key point is that the premises don't state that all things that suck are vampires. The premises only state that vampires suck, but that's a one-way implication. So just because something sucks, it doesn't mean it's a vampire. Therefore, space being a vacuum might \"suck,\" but that doesn't make it a vampire. So the statement \"Space is a vampire\" would be false, because there's no logical connection between being a vacuum and being a vampire.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is trying to trick us into thinking that since both vampires and vacuums \"suck,\" then space (being a vacuum) is a vampire. But that's a faulty syllogism. Just because A and B have a common property doesn't mean A is B.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The context says \"Vampires suck.\" That could mean that all vampires have the trait of sucking (blood). \"Vacuums suck\" (appliances) meaning they have suction. \"Space is a vacuum,\" so space is a vacuum (the physical state), so does that mean space \"sucks\" in the same way a vacuum cleaner does? Maybe not exactly. A vacuum cleaner creates suction, but space as a vacuum is just empty. So maybe space doesn't actually \"suck,\" but is a vacuum. The original premises say \"Vacuums suck,\" which refers to the appliances, not the physical state. Wait, the wording is a bit ambiguous. Let me parse the exact wording again.\n\nThe context says:\n\n\"Plungers suck. Vacuums suck. Vampires suck. Space is a vacuum. A duster is a household appliance that doesn't suck.\"\n\nSo \"vacuums suck\" is referring to vacuum cleaners, since those are the appliances that suck. Then \"Space is a vacuum\" \u2014 here, \"vacuum\" is the noun meaning empty space, so space is an example of a vacuum (the 