messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\n"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. Karen does not download "Black Mirror". "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? "Black Mirror" is popular.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\n:.\nOkay, let\'s see.I need to figure out if "Black Mirror" is popular based on the given information.Let me go through each statement one by one and see what I can deduce.\n\nFirst, the context says that "Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show.Then, there\'s a conditional: if a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.So, popularity implies binge-watching by Karen..\n\nNext, it says that Karen will download a Netflix show if and only if she binge-watches it.That\'s an "if and only if" statement, which means both directions.So, she downloads it only if she binge-watched, and if she binge-watched, she must have downloaded it.So, downloading and binge-watching are equivalent here.\n\nThen, we\'re told that Karen does not download "Black Mirror".Since "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show, that\'s given too..\n\nThe last statement is that if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it with Lisa..\n\nThe question is whether "Black Mirror" is popular.The options are True, False, Uncertain.\n\nLet me structure the statements symbolically to make it clearer.\n\nLet me denote:.\n\nLet P(X) = "X is popular".\n\nB(X) = "Karen binge-watches X".\n\nD(X) = "Karen downloads X".\n\nS(X) = "Karen shares X with Lisa".\n\nGiven:.\n\n1.P("Stranger Things") – given directly.\n\n2.For any Netflix show X, if P(X) then B(X).So P(X) → B(X).\n\n3.For any Netflix show X, B(X) ↔ D(X).So B(X) if and only if D(X).\n\n4.¬D("Black Mirror") – Karen does not download Black Mirror.\n\n5."Black Mirror" is a Netflix show.\n\n6.For any Netflix show X, if B(X) then S(X).So B(X) → S(X).\n\nWe need to determine if P("Black Mirror") is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nStarting with Black Mirror (let\'s abbreviate as BM).We know BM is a Netflix show.From statement 4, ¬D(BM).Since B ↔ D, then ¬D(BM) implies ¬B(BM).Because if D were true, then B would have to be true, but since D is false, B must be false.\n\nSo, since B(BM) is false, then from the contrapositive of statement 2 (which is P→B, so contrapositive is ¬B → ¬P), we can conclude that ¬P(BM).Therefore, "Black Mirror" is popular.That would make the statement "Black Mirror is popular" true.So the answer should be True.\n\nWait, let me check again.The contrapositive of P→B is indeed ¬B→¬P.Since we have ¬B(BM), that leads to ¬P(BM).Therefore, the statement "Black Mirror is popular" is true.Therefore the answer is True.\n\nIs there any possibility I missed something?.\n\nLet me verify each step again:.\n\n1.BM is a Netflix show.Given.\n\n2.Karen does not download BM.Given.\n\n3.Since D(X) ↔ B(X), then not D(X) implies not B(X).So, since D(BM) is false, B(BM) must be false.\n\n4.From the first conditional: if a Netflix show is popular (P(X)), then Karen binge-watches it (B(X)).So P(X) → B(X).The contrapositive is ¬B(X) → ¬P(X).Therefore, since B(BM) is false, then P(BM) must be false.So "Black Mirror" is popular.Hence the statement is true.So the answer is True.\n\nI don\'t see any gaps here.The key steps are the biconditional between binge and download, leading to the negation of binge, then using the contrapositive of the first implication.The other statements about sharing with Lisa aren\'t directly relevant here, unless there\'s some hidden connection, but since the question is about popularity of BM, the sharing part doesn\'t affect that.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
