      "text": " A) True? Wait wait wait, wait a second. Wait, if P(X) \u2192 B(X), so popularity implies binge. But the contrapositive is \u00acB \u2192 \u00acP. So if Karen didn't binge, then it's not popular. Therefore, if BM is not binge-watched, then it is not popular. Wait wait, hold on. Wait I think I made a mistake here. Let me re-express this.\n\nWait, the user is asking if \"Black Mirror is popular\" is true. But according to the logic above, since \u00acB(BM) \u2192 \u00acP(BM). So that means that BM is not popular. Therefore the statement \"Black Mirror is popular\" would be false. Wait, that's the opposite of what I thought earlier! Wait I must have messed up here. Let me go through again.\n\nLet me start over to clarify:\n\nThe question is: Is \"Black Mirror\" popular?\n\nGiven that Karen does not download BM (\u00acD(BM)), and since D(X) \u2194 B(X), then \u00acD(BM) \u2194 \u00acB(BM). So \u00acB(BM).\n\nNow, the first statement: if a Netflix show is popular (P(X)), then Karen binge-watches it (B(X)). So P(X) \u2192 B(X).\n\nTherefore, if the show were popular, Karen would have binge-watched it. But since Karen did not binge-watch it (\u00acB(BM)), then it cannot be the case that P(BM) is true. Because if it were, then B would have to be true. So P(BM) must be false. Therefore the statement \"Black Mirror is popular\" is false. Therefore the correct answer is B) False.\n\nOh no! I see where I made the mistake earlier. Initially I thought the contrapositive led to the conclusion that it was popular, but that's the opposite. I must have confused the direction. Let me verify again:\n\nIf P \u2192 B, then the contrapositive is \u00acB \u2192 \u00acP. So if Karen didn't binge, then it's not popular. Therefore, since \u00acB(BM) is true, then \u00acP(BM) must be true. Thus the statement \"Black Mirror is popular\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, that's a critical error. So the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, let me check again step by step:\n\nGiven that Karen doesn't download BM \u2192 so \u00acD(BM). Since D(X) \u2194 B(X), that means that \u00acB(BM). So Karen doesn't binge BM.\n\nNow, the first rule: if a show is popular (P(X)), then Karen binges it (B(X)). So P(X) \u2192 B(X). So if it were popular, then Karen would have to have binged it. But since she didn't, then the show cannot be popular. Therefore, P(BM) is false. Hence the statement \"Black Mirror is popular\" is false. So the answer is B.\n\nAh, so my initial thought was wrong. The correct answer is B) False. I must have mixed up the contrapositive direction earlier. So important to be careful here.\n\nLet me see if there's any other angle. Suppose someone argues that maybe there's another way for BM to be popular without Karen binge-watching? But according to the first rule, if it's popular, then Karen must binge it. So being popular necessarily leads to binge-watching. Since she didn't, it's impossible for it to be popular. So the conclusion is that it's false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires determining whether \"Black Mirror\" is popular based on the given conditions. Here's the breakdown:\n\n1. **Key Statements**:\n   - If a Netflix show is popular (P(X)), Karen binge-watches it (B(X)).\n   - Karen downloads a show (D(X)) **if and only if** she binge-watches it (B(X)).  \n   - Karen does not download \"Black Mirror\" (\u00acD(\"Black Mirror\")).  \n   - \"Black Mirror\" is a Netflix show.  \n\n2. **Logical Deductions**:\n   - Since Karen does not download \"Black Mirror\" (\u00acD), the biconditional (B \u2194 D) implies she also does not binge-watch it (\u00acB).  \n   - The contrapositive of \"If a show is popular (P), Karen binge-watches it (B)\" is **\u00acB \u2192 \u00acP**. Thus, \u00acB(\"Black Mirror\") leads to \u00acP(\"Black Mirror\").  \n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - \"Black Mirror\" cannot be popular because its unpopularity is logically necessary given that Karen did not binge-watch it.  \n\n**Answer**: B) False",
