messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf a player is ranked highly by the Women's Tennis Association, then they are among the most active players in major tennis. Everyone who lost to Iga Świątek is ranked highly by the Women's Tennis Association. All female tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 lost to Iga Świątek. Either female tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 or male tennis players at Roland Garros 2022. All male tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 lost to Rafael Nadal. If Coco Gauff is a player who is ranked highly by the Women's Tennis Association or a player who lost to Rafael Nadal, then Coco Gauff is not a male tennis player at Roland Garros 2022.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Coco Gauff is not a player who lost to Iga Świątek or one of the most active players in major tennis.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain", '<think>\n:.\nOkay, let\'s see.I need to figure out whether the statement "Coco Gauff is not a player who lost to Iga Świątek or one of the most active players in major tennis" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given information.Hmm.Let me break down the context step by step.\n\nFirst, let me parse all the given statements one by one to understand the relationships.\n\n1.**If a player is ranked highly by the WTA, then they are among the most active players in major tennis.**.\n   So, High WTA Rank → Most Active in Major Tennis.\n\n2.**Everyone who lost to Iga Świątek is ranked highly by the WTA.**.\n   So, Lost to Iga → High WTA Rank.\n\n3.**All female tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 lost to Iga Świątek.**.\n   So, Female at RG2022 → Lost to Iga.\n\n4.**Either female tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 or male tennis players at Roland Garros 2022.**.\n   Wait, this is a bit confusing.Maybe it\'s saying that every tennis player at Roland Garros 2022 is either female or male?Or perhaps that either all female players are there, or all male?Wait, maybe the original is saying that the players are partitioned into female or male, so every player is either one or the other.Probably the former, that all players are either female or male, so that the set of players at RG2022 is divided into those two groups.So, any player at RG2022 is either female or male.That makes sense.\n\n5.**All male tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 lost to Rafael Nadal.**.\n   So, Male at RG2022 → Lost to Rafael.\n\n6.**If Coco Gauff is a player who is ranked highly by the WTA or a player who lost to Rafael Nadal, then Coco Gauff is not a male tennis player at Roland Garros 2022.**.\n   So, (High WTA Rank ∨ Lost to Rafael) → ¬Male at RG2022.\n\nNow, the question is about Coco Gauff.The statement to evaluate is: "Coco Gauff is not a player who lost to Iga Świątek or one of the most active players in major tennis.".\n\nWait, the statement is "not (lost to Iga or most active)".So, logically, that would be equivalent to "¬ (Lost to Iga ∨ Most Active)".Which by De Morgan\'s laws is equivalent to "¬Lost to Iga ∧ ¬Most Active".\n\nBut the question is whether this statement is true, false, or uncertain.So we need to see if we can prove that Coco Gauff is neither someone who lost to Iga nor among the most active, or if it\'s impossible, or if we can\'t tell.\n\nLet me consider Coco Gauff\'s possible status.First, we need to figure out if Coco is a female or male player at RG2022, or not a player there at all?.\n\nBut the context might not specify that.Wait, the statements are about players at RG2022.Let me see.\n\nFirst, let\'s note that Coco Gauff could be a female player at RG2022, or a male player there, or maybe not even participating.Since the context mentions "either female or male" players at RG2022, but doesn\'t say that all players are there.Hmm, actually statement 4 might be a bit ambiguous.Let me recheck:.\n\nStatement 4 says: "Either female tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 or male tennis players at Roland Garros 2022."Wait, maybe it\'s saying that for any tennis player at RG2022, they are either female or male.So, every player is in one of those two categories.So, the participants are partitioned into female and male.So, Coco could be a participant (either female or male) or not a participant.The problem doesn\'t say Coco is a participant, so we have to consider possibilities.\n\nBut let\'s see the question is about Coco Gauff in general.The statement is about whether she is not someone who lost to Iga or most active.Let me try to proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s consider possible scenarios for Coco Gauff.\n\nCase 1: Suppose Coco is a female tennis player at Roland Garros 2022.\n\nThen, by statement 3, she must have lost to Iga Świątek.Because all female players there lost to Iga.So, if she\'s female at RG2022, then she lost to Iga.Therefore, she would be someone who lost to Iga.Then, the statement we need to evaluate is "Coco is not (lost to Iga or most active)".But since she is lost to Iga, then the "lost to Iga" part is true, so the negation would be false.Therefore, in this case, the statement would be false.\n\nBut wait, but there\'s more.Since she lost to Iga, then by statement 2, she is ranked highly by WTA.And then by statement 1, she is among the most active.So, if she\'s a female at RG2022, then she both lost to Iga and is most active.So the statement "not (lost to Iga or most active)" would be false because both are true.So the statement would be false.\n\nCase 2: Suppose Coco is a male tennis player at Roland Garros 2022.\n\nThen, by statement 5, all male players there lost to Rafael Nadal.So Coco would have lost to Rafael.Also, statement 6 says that if Coco is either highly ranked by WTA or lost to Rafael, then she is not a male player at RG2022.Wait, but in this case, she is a male player and she lost to Rafael (so the antecedent is true because she lost to Rafael).Therefore, the conclusion would be that she is not a male player, which is a contradiction.Therefore, this case cannot hold.Therefore, Coco cannot be a male player at RG2022.\n\nTherefore, if Coco is a male at RG2022, that leads to a contradiction.So she can\'t be a male there.\n\nCase 3: Suppose Coco is not a player at Roland Garros 2022 at all.So she is neither female nor male there.\n\nThen, we have to consider her status.Let\'s see.\n\nIs there any information about Coco Gauff outside of RG2022?The problem doesn\'t say.The statements only talk about players at RG2022.So maybe she could be a WTA player not at RG2022, or not?.\n\nWait, the first statement is general: if a player is ranked highly by WTA, then they are among most active in major tennis.So regardless of where they are, that holds.So if Coco is a highly ranked WTA player, then she is among the most active.But if she\'s not, then maybe not.\n\nBut the question is about whether she is not (lost to Iga or most active).Let\'s see.\n\nIf she is not at RG2022, then she didn\'t lose to Iga (since all who lost to Iga are the female players at RG2022, per statement 3).So "lost to Iga" would be false.Then, whether she is among the most active?If she is a highly ranked WTA player, then yes, she would be.But if she is not, then she might not be.\n\nBut we don\'t know if Coco is highly ranked.The only info about Coco is in statement 6: If she is highly ranked or lost to Rafael, then she is not a male at RG2022.Since we already saw she can\'t be a male there, but this is a conditional.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Coco could be a highly ranked player not at RG2022.Let\'s suppose she is highly ranked.Then, by statement 1, she is among the most active.Therefore, the statement "not (lost to Iga or most active)" would be "not (false or true)" which is false.So the statement would be false.\n\nAlternatively, if she is not highly ranked, then maybe she isn\'t among the most active.But then, the "lost to Iga" part would also be false (since she\'s not at RG2022).So "lost to Iga or most active" would be false, so the negation would be true.Wait, that\'s a different scenario.\n\nWait, this is getting a bit tangled.Let me try to structure this.\n\nFirst, let\'s consider all possibilities for Coco\'s status:.\n\n1.She is a female player at RG2022: Then, as above, she lost to Iga (so the first part is true), so the negation is false.So the statement is false.\n\n2.She is a male player at RG2022: Impossible because that leads to a contradiction (statement 6).\n\n3.She is not at RG2022 at all: So she is either a female not there, a male not there, or not a player?Let\'s see.\n\nWait, the problem says "female tennis players at Roland Garros 2022" so Coco could be a female not there, or a male not there, but the statements don\'t restrict her outside of that.\n\nSo, if she\'s not at RG2022, then:.\n\n- Whether she is highly ranked: If she is, then she is among most active (from statement 1).So "lost to Iga" is false, but "most active" is true.Thus, the statement "not (lost to Iga OR most active)" is false.\n\n- If she is not highly ranked, then maybe she is not among the most active.But then, "lost to Iga" is false (since she wasn\'t at RG2022), so "lost to Iga OR most active" would be false (since both parts are false).Then, the negation would be true.So in that case, the statement would be true.\n\nBut we don\'t know if Coco is highly ranked or not.The only info is in statement 6: If Coco is highly ranked OR lost to Rafael, then she is not a male at RG2022.Since she can\'t be a male there anyway, this doesn\'t give us info about her being highly ranked or lost to Rafael.Wait, but if she\'s not at RG2022, then losing to Rafael would require her to be a male player there, which she can\'t be.Wait, losing to Rafael is only for male players at RG2022 (since statement 5 says all male players there lost to Rafael).So if Coco is not a male at RG2022, then she can\'t have lost to Rafael.Unless there\'s another way?Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe losing to Rafael could happen outside of RG2022?The statements don\'t specify that Rafael\'s matches are only at RG2022.Hmm, but the context mentions "male tennis players at Roland Garros 2022 lost to Rafael Nadal".So maybe the only info is that those males at RG2022 lost to Rafael, but Coco could have lost to him elsewhere.But the problem doesn\'t state that.Since the problem is about logical reasoning based on given info, we can\'t assume anything beyond the given statements.\n\nTherefore, Coco\'s loss to Rafael would only be possible if she were a male at RG2022, which is impossible.Therefore, Coco cannot have lost to Rafael.Therefore, the antecedent in statement 6 (High WTA or Lost to Rafael) would only be true if she is highly ranked.So, if she is highly ranked, then statement 6 says she is not a male at RG2022.But since she\'s not a male there anyway, that doesn\'t add info.So statement 6 only tells us that if she is highly ranked or lost to Rafael (which she can\'t have done), then she isn\'t a male there, which we already know.\n\nTherefore, we can\'t determine from the given info whether Coco is highly ranked or not.Therefore, in case 3 (not at RG2022), there are two possibilities:.\n\na) She is highly ranked: Then she is most active, so the statement\'s negation would be false.\n\nb) She is not highly ranked: Then she is not most active, and since she didn\'t lose to Iga, the negation would be true.\n\nTherefore, depending on her ranking, the statement could be true or false.Therefore, we can\'t determine it definitively.So that would make the answer uncertain (C).But wait, let\'s check again.\n\nWait, but in case 3a, if she is highly ranked, then by statement 1, she is among the most active.So the statement "Coco is not (lost to Iga or most active)" would be "not (False OR True)" which is False.So the statement would be false in that case.\n\nIn case 3b, if she is not highly ranked, then she is not among the most active (since the first statement only says that high rank implies most active, but not the converse).So "most active" could be false.Therefore, "lost to Iga OR most active" would be "False OR False", so the negation is True.So the statement would be true in that case.\n\nTherefore, since we don\'t know whether Coco is highly ranked or not, the truth of the statement depends on that, which isn\'t given.Therefore, it\'s uncertain.So the answer would be C.The answer is certain.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
