can\'t have any wedding at all, that\'s impossible.Therefore, the answer is B) False..\n\nBut hold on, let me check if there\'s a different interpretation of premise 5.The fifth statement says:.\n\n"If John has at least one child, then John does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding.".\n\nSo, that\'s HasChild → (¬ParisHoneymoon ∧ ¬Wedding).So, if he has a child, then he cannot have a wedding.But premise 1 says that having a wedding requires having a child.So, the only way for John to have a wedding is if he has a child, but that would then prevent him from having a wedding.Therefore, the only consistent state is that John does not have a wedding (so he cannot have a child via premise 1, unless he has a wedding, which he can\'t).Wait, but maybe John could have a child without having a wedding?.\n\nWait, premise 1 says that all people who have a wedding have at least one child, but it doesn\'t say the converse.So, someone could have a child without having a wedding.So, premise 1 is only one direction.So, John could have a child without having a wedding.John\'s wedding is not well-attended. If he did have a well-attended wedding, he would either have it in July or go to Paris. But since he can\'t have a wedding at all, this is a vacuous truth. The answer is A) True.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
