      "text": " (The final answer is B) False.\n\nWait, hold on, let's retrace.\n\nWait, in case 1, when C is true, then the conclusion Y \u2227 I requires Y and I. Since she's inactive (I) but not Y, the conclusion is false. Therefore, the implication (C \u2228 P) \u2192 (Y \u2227 I) would be false in that case. Therefore, the statement is false, so the answer would be B) False.\n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, in case 1, Bonnie is chaperoning (C). From premise 2 (P \u2228 I), since P is false (because if P were true, then A would have to be true, but A would require S which is false, leading contradiction), so P is false, so I must be true. Therefore, Bonnie is I. Now, the conclusion requires Y and I. Since Bonnie is I, but is she Y?\n\nY is \"young child or teenager who wishes to further their academic...\" So, if Bonnie is Y, then from premise 5, Y \u2192 S. But Bonnie is not a student (\u00acS), so Y must be false. Therefore, Bonnie is not Y. Therefore, the conclusion Y \u2227 I is false. So the implication is \"If antecedent (which is true in this case), then conclusion (false)\", which makes the implication false. Therefore, the entire statement is false.\n\nNow, what about case 2, where Bonnie doesn't chaperone (\u00acC), but she performs often (P). So P is true. Then from premise 1, P \u2192 A, so A is true. From Bonnie's condition (A \u2194 S), since A is true, S must be true (she's a student). Now, we need to see whether she is Y and I. The conclusion is Y \u2227 I.\n\nFirst, since she is a student (S), from premise 5's converse? Wait, premise 5 is Y \u2192 S, but the converse isn't necessarily true. So being a student doesn't mean she is Y. So she could be a student but not Y, or Y and thus a student. But we need to see if she can be Y and I?\n\nWait, let's see. Since she is performing often (P), from premise 2 (P \u2228 I), so if P is true, then I can be either true or false? Wait, no. The premise is P \u2228 I must