      "text": " If the consequent is false, then James must have H and \u00acR, or \u00acH and R. Because those are the cases not covered by the consequent.\n\nWait, let's re-express the negation of the consequent:\n\n\u00ac[ (H \u2227 R) \u2228 (\u00acH \u2227 \u00acR) ]\n\n= \u00ac(H \u2227 R) \u2227 \u00ac(\u00acH \u2227 \u00acR)\n\n= (\u00acH \u2228 \u00acR) \u2227 (H \u2228 R)\n\nSo this is the case when H and R are not both true, and not both false. So exactly one of H and R is true.\n\nSo the consequent is false only when exactly one of H or R holds.\n\nTherefore, to see if there's a case where (M\u2228C) is true but the consequent is false (i.e., H XOR R is true), we need to check if such a scenario is possible under the premises.\n\nLet me see:\n\nCase 1: James is a manager (M).\n\nEarlier analysis showed that if M is true, then H must be false (because H\u2192R, but M\u2192\u00acR, so H can't be true). Also, R must be false. Therefore, H is false, R is false. So both are false, so the consequent's second part (\u00acH \u2227 \u00acR) is true, so the consequent holds. So no problem here.\n\nCase 2: James is in other countries (C is true, M is false).\n\nAs analyzed, in that case, he must have H=true and R=true, so (H \u2227 R) is true, so the consequent holds.\n\nAnother possibility: Could there be a scenario where C is true but H is false and R is true? Well, R is true via C, so if H is false, then H is false and R is true. That would mean H and R are not both true or both false. So that would make the consequent false.\n\nWait, but in this scenario:\n\nJames is in other countries (C=true, so R=true), and he's not a manager (so M is false).\n\nWe need to see if he can have lunch at the company (H is false, L is true).\n\nBut earlier, we saw that if he has lunch at the company (L), then he must schedule a meeting (S), leading to A (appearing in company). But since he is in other countries, does that mean he can't appear? Wait, the problem says \"All employees who schedule meetings will appear in the company today\". So if he schedules a meeting (S), he must appear (A). But if he is in other countries, does that prevent him from appearing? The premises don't explicitly say that being in another country means he can't be here. The only thing is that if he is in another country, he works remotely (R). Working remotely might mean not appearing in the company. But the first premise says that if you schedule a meeting, you must appear. So if he schedules a meeting, even if he works remotely, he must appear? That would be a conflict.\n\nWait, let me re-express:\n\nIf he is in other countries (C), then he works remotely (R). Working remotely usually implies not being present physically. But the first premise requires that if he schedules a meeting (S), he must appear in the company (A). So if he's working remotely (R), but he also has to appear in the company (A), that could be conflicting, unless working remotely doesn't prevent him from appearing. Hmm, but perhaps the company allows remote employees to attend meetings in person? Or maybe the presence is required for the meeting.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the conclusion that H must be true is correct because if he has lunch at the company (L), then he must schedule a meeting (S), which would require him to appear (A). But since he is in other countries, does that make A impossible? The problem doesn't state that being in another country means he can't appear. So perhaps he could be in another country but still come to the company for the day? That might be possible, but then he would have to work remotely from home, conflicting with being present.\n\nWait, the problem states \"All employees who are in other countries work remotely from home.\" So if he's in another country (C), he must work remotely (R). But working remotely (R) is defined as working from home, I assume. So if he's working remotely from home, he is at home, so he can't be at the company. Therefore, if he is in another country and working remotely (R), then he can't be appearing in the company (A is false).\n\nBut from premise 7, if he is not a manager (since M is false when C is true), then he must be in \u00acM \u2227 \u00acA. So that requires \u00acA, which aligns with his remote work.\n\nHowever, if he has lunch at the company (L), then he must have scheduled a meeting (S), which requires him to appear in the company (A). But he can't appear (\u00acA). Therefore, he can't have L. Thus, he must have H. Therefore, in the case of C being true, he must have H and R both true. So the consequent holds.\n\nTherefore, there is no scenario where (M\u2228C) is true but the consequent is false. Therefore, the implication must always hold, so the statement is true.\n\nWait, but let me check another angle: What if James is a manager and in other countries? Wait, that's impossible because as a manager, he can't work remotely (M\u2192\u00acR), but being in other countries (C\u2192R), so R would have to be both true and false. So such a scenario is impossible.\n\nTherefore, whenever (M\u2228C) is true, the consequent holds. Therefore, the statement is true. So the answer should be A) True.\n\nWait a second, but let me check all possibilities again.\n\nSuppose James is a manager (so M is true). Then:\n\n- From premise 7, he must be (M \u2227 A).\n\n- So he must appear (A is true).\n\n- From the first premise, if he schedules a meeting (S), then he appears, which is okay, but he could choose not to schedule.\n\n- But his lunch: he must choose between L or H.\n\n- If he chooses L (lunch at company), then he must schedule a meeting (S), but that's okay, and he appears.\n\n- If he chooses H (lunch at home), then he must work remotely (R). But since he's a manager, that's impossible (M\u2192\u00acR). Therefore, he can't choose H. So he must choose L. So H is false. Therefore:\n\n- H is false, R is false (since he can't work remote as a manager).\n\n- Thus, the consequent (H \u2227 R) \u222