 contrapositive is ¬VisitOfficeSupply → ¬FullyStockedOffice.But the conclusion\'s implication is VisitOfficeSupply → ¬FullyStockedOffice, which is the opposite direction.That would mean that visiting the store implies not having a fully stocked office, but the premises only tell us that having a fully stocked office requires visiting the store.So someone who visits the store could still have a fully stocked office or not.The implication in the conclusion would require that visiting the store precludes having a fully stocked office, which is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nSuppose Remy is in Case 2 (so ¬TidyOrganize and ¬(LateBed ∧ NoonWake)), and also visits the office supply store regularly (VisitOfficeSupply).The question is, can Remy have a FullyStockedOffice?\n\nIf Remy has FullyStockedOffice, then by Premise 2, they must own a monitor (OwnMonitor).Then by Premise 1, they must WorkAtHome.If they WorkAtHome, then by Premise 3, they must TidyOrganize.But Remy is in Case 2 where ¬TidyOrganize.Therefore, if Remy had FullyStockedOffice, that would lead to a contradiction because they would have to TidyOrganize but they don\'t.Therefore, Remy cannot have FullyStockedOffice.\n\nWait, this is a key point.Let me walk through this again.\n\nIf Remy has a FullyStockedOffice (FullyStockedOffice is true):\n\n- From Premise2: FullyStockedOffice → OwnMonitor → WorkAtHome (Premise1)\n- WorkAtHome (from Premise3) → TidyOrganize\n- But Remy is in Case 2, so ¬TidyOrganize.Contradiction.Therefore, Remy cannot have FullyStockedOffice if they are in Case2.\n\nTherefore, if Remy is in Case2 (which they must be, since Case1 is impossible), then if they VisitOfficeSupply, does that affect anything?\n\nWait, but the VisitOfficeSupply could be for other reasons not related to working at home.Wait, but according to the premises, the only way someone visits the office supply store is if they work at home (Premise4: WorkAtHome → VisitOfficeSupply).However, the converse isn\'t stated.So maybe there are people who visit the store without working at home?The premises don\'t say that only workers visit the store, so VisitOfficeSupply could be true for other reasons.\n\nBut in Remy\'s case, since Remy is in Case2 (¬(LateBed ∧ NoonWake) and ¬TidyOrganize), can Remy visit the store?\n\nIf Remy visits the store (VisitOfficeSupply), then there are two possibilities:\n\n1.Remy works at home (WorkAtHome), which would require TidyOrganize (Premise3), but Remy is in Case2 which has ¬TidyOrganize.Contradiction.So this can\'t be.\n\n2.Remy doesn\'t work at home, but still visits the store for some other reason.Since the premises don\'t prohibit that, this is possible.\n\nTherefore, Remy could visit the store without working at home.But if Remy is visiting the store and is in Case2, does that mean they can have a FullyStockedOffice?\n\nWait, let\'s see:\n\nSuppose Remy has FullyStockedOffice.Then, as before, they must WorkAtHome (via Premise2 and 1).But that would require TidyOrganize (Premise3), which Remy doesn\'t have.So having FullyStockedOffice is impossible for Remy.Therefore, regardless of why Remy is visiting the store, Remy cannot have FullyStockedOffice because that would require TidyOrganize, which they don\'t have.Therefore, if Remy is in Case2 and visits the store, they still can\'t have a FullyStockedOffice, so the consequent (¬TidyOrganize ∧ ¬FullyStockedOffice) would hold because both parts are true (since ¬TidyOrganize is already true, and ¬FullyStockedOffice is also true).Therefore, the implication holds in this case.\n\nWait, but let me check again.Let me suppose that Remy is in Case2 (so ¬Tidy and ¬(LateBed ∧ Noon)), and suppose that Remy visits the store (VisitOfficeSupply).Then, does that mean Remy can\'t have a FullyStockedOffice?\n\nNo, Remy can easily have a FullyStockedOffice without needing to WorkAtHome or TidyOrganize, contradicting the logic in Case2.Therefore, the implication\'s consequent is false, meaning the implication 