t Rose is young or a student. Could that be relevant?\n\nHmm, Rose's status might not relate to Jerry unless there's some general rule inferred. For example, if being young is connected to being a student, but the problem says \"Rose is young or a student\", which is an inclusive OR. So Rose could be both young and a student, or either/or. But that doesn't impose any restriction on others.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a rule that all young people are students? But the problem doesn't say that. The only thing is that young people can't teach, but they can be students or not? Wait, but if a young person is a student, that's allowed, but there's no requirement.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if someone is a student, then they must be young? No, that's not stated. So students can be any age.\n\nTherefore, since Jerry is a student, but age isn't restricted, so we can't determine.\n\nAnother angle: Since all students are humans (premise 1), which is trivial, but maybe not helpful.\n\nWait, perhaps there's an implicit assumption that only young people are students? But that's not stated. The problem doesn't say that. So we can't assume that.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that it's uncertain whether Jerry is young. So answer C.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Maybe there's another angle.\n\nWait, premise 3 says everyone either studies or teaches. Since Jerry studies, so he doesn't teach. Now, if someone is a student, are there any constraints? Like, can a student be a teacher? If a student can also teach, then maybe a student who teaches would be allowed, but since Jerry doesn't teach, that's not an issue.\n\nAlternatively, if a student can't teach, but that's not stated. The only constraint is that young people can't teach.\n\nWait, but if a student is allowed to teach unless they're young, then that's okay. So a non-young student could teach? Wait, but premise 3 says everyone must study or teach. So if someone is a student (so they might study or not?), but actually, if 